@startuml architecture-microservices
title Cinema System - Current Local Microservices Architecture

top to bottom direction
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam shadowing false

actor "Cinema staff" as Staff

node "Local development computer" {
    component "Web browser" as Browser

    node "Website process\nlocalhost:5300" {
        component "CinemaSystem.Web\nASP.NET MVC website" as Website
    }

    node "Booking service process\nlocalhost:5209" {
        component "CinemaSystem.Booking.Api\nCinema management and bookings" as Booking
        component "CinemaSystem.Core\nTicket creators and ticket classes" as Core
    }

    node "Pricing service process\nlocalhost:5104" {
        component "CinemaSystem.Pricing.Api\nDynamic pricing and ticket strategies" as Pricing
    }

    database "cinema.db\nSQLite" as Database
}

Staff --> Browser : uses
Browser --> Website : HTTP requests\nHTML pages and forms
Website --> Booking : HTTP / JSON\nMovies, screens, showings,\nquotes and bookings
Booking --> Pricing : HTTP / JSON\nPOST /api/pricing/quote
Booking --> Database : Entity Framework Core\nReads and writes
Booking ..> Core : in-process library calls

note right of Website
Controllers call the Booking API.
Views display the returned data.
No direct database access.
end note

note right of Booking
Facade coordinates booking operations.
Uses Factory Method ticket creators.
Owns cinema and booking data.
end note

note right of Pricing
Strategy selects ticket-type pricing.
Applies premium and demand adjustments.
Stateless: no database access.
end note

note bottom of Database
Movies, Screens, Showings,
Bookings and BookedTickets
end note

legend bottom
Solid arrows show runtime communication.
The dotted arrow shows use of a local library.
Website, Booking and Pricing run as separate processes.
Core is a library, not a microservice.
Azure hosting is not yet implemented.
endlegend

@enduml